1:# Claims and evidence map
2:
3:Use this for real-world facts, Minecraft mechanics, quoted material, cultural references, licences, and business claims.
4:
5:## Supported claims
6:
7:### C-001 — Participation versus alteration in a single past
8:
9:**Claim:** In a model with one past, a traveller cannot make an established past event different, but may participate in and help cause that event.
10:
11:**Support:** SRC-001, Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, substantive revision 22 March 2024.
12:
13:**Use in project:** This distinction supports a protagonist whose decisions remain causally important even though the observed destruction cannot be rewritten.
14:
15:**Boundary:** This is a philosophical account of a coherent model, not proof that backwards time travel exists.
16:
17:### C-002 — Self-consistency as a proposed constraint
18:
19:**Claim:** A published closed-timelike-curve model proposes that locally possible events must extend into a globally self-consistent history.
20:
21:**Support:** SRC-002, Friedman et al., *Physical Review D* 42(6), 1990, DOI 10.1103/PhysRevD.42.1915.
22:
23:**Use in project:** This informs the fictional rule that every portal crossing belongs to one history rather than producing a branch or reset.
24:
25:**Boundary:** The paper is conditional theoretical work. It does not demonstrate a real time machine and does not validate the fictional portal mechanism.
26:
27:## Story facts versus factual claims
28:
29:**Proposed story facts (invented, unlocked canon):**
30:
31:- The portal can connect different moments of the same Minecraft world.
32:- There is one self-consistent timeline rather than parallel worlds or repeated resets.
33:- The older protagonist physically constructs the portal before his younger self discovers it.
34:- The protagonist witnesses the burned future of his homestead and initially believes his wolf was lost.
35:- The homestead’s destruction remains fixed.
36:- The protagonist’s rescue of the wolf was always part of the observed history.
37:- The time-travel effect is speculative fiction and is not presented as a vanilla Minecraft mechanic.
38:
39:**Factual claims about philosophy or physics:**
40:
41:- C-001 and C-002 only, with the limitations recorded above.
42:
43:**Minecraft behaviours still requiring evidence:**
44:
45:- The exact reproducible mechanic that destroys the homestead.
46:- How the protagonist’s attempted safeguard interacts with that mechanic.
47:- Fire spread, portal behaviour, animal movement and capture reliability in the production version of Minecraft.
48:- The practical capture method for showing two temporal versions of one avatar.
49:
50:These behaviours are not yet asserted as supported claims. They must be tested or sourced after the Story phase selects the causal chain.
51:
52:## Conflicts and uncertainty
53:
54:The two sources do not conflict at the limited level used: both permit discussion of internally consistent histories. They approach the subject from different disciplines and neither establishes that backwards time travel is physically achievable.
55:
56:A causal loop can raise a deeper authorship question even when every physical object has an ordinary origin. The story may preserve that question as thematic residue, but it must not use it to excuse an untraceable Minecraft object or a missing causal step.
57:
58:The homestead, fire and wolf are a concrete direction proposal, not locked canon. Their dramatic value and production feasibility require human review before `direction_lock`.
59:
60:## Research exclusions
61:
62:- No existing film, television episode, novel, game narrative or online plot summary was used as a story template.
63:- No quotations or copyrighted passages will appear in the video from these sources.
64:- Branching-timeline and many-worlds models were excluded because they contradict the requested fixed outcome.
65:- Claims that time travel is scientifically proven or practically achievable were excluded.
66:- Version-specific Minecraft claims remain excluded until a mechanics test or source pass is completed.

